# The golden expectations are records of bytes, not source files.
#
# Each file under tests/golden/expected/ holds exactly what the tool produced: a
# sample artefact is CRLF because that is what the csv module writes on every
# platform, and a recorded stream is LF because that is what the normalisation
# produces. Both are the point of the comparison.
#
# Git's line-ending conversion cannot tell those apart from an ordinary text file.
# With core.autocrlf on, checking out turns the LF streams into CRLF and a fresh
# clone on another machine reports every stream case as a mismatch; storing a
# sample artefact turns its CRLF into LF, and a fresh clone on Linux compares LF
# against the CRLF the tool actually wrote. Neither failure has anything to do
# with the tool.
#
# So these files are stored and checked out byte for byte, on every platform.
tests/golden/expected/** -text

# HL7's FHIR R4 XML schema set, fetched by examples/fhir/fetch.py and committed here.
#
# The same reason as above, arrived at the other way round: these are third-party files
# that this repository does not author, and it does not edit. What they are is a record
# of bytes -- HL7's, published under their own licence -- and the reason this repository
# stores them at all is that tests/integration/test_xsd_namespaces.py compiles all 150 of
# them, which it could not do while they lived in data/ (gitignored, fetched per machine).
#
# Measured on the set fetched 2026-10-01: 136 files are CRLF throughout, 2 are LF
# throughout and 12 are mixed within one file, and three begin with a UTF-8 BOM
# (fhir-all.xsd, fhir-xhtml.xsd, xml.xsd). So the endings are not uniform and not
# guessable, and with core.autocrlf on an unmarked checkout would rewrite 136 of them on
# Windows and normalise the three BOMs away -- leaving files that no longer match the
# per-file sha256 values recorded in examples/fhir/fhir.json, on whichever platform
# happens to disagree with the one that fetched them.
#
# `-text` stores and checks out every one of these byte for byte, on every platform, so
# the recorded digests describe the same bytes everywhere. It changes nothing about what
# is committed, and a file here must not be edited: to update the set, re-fetch it.
examples/fhir/xsd/** -text
